= Nonviolent Communication — Simple =

<onlyinclude>'''Nonviolent Communication''' ('''NVC''') is a way of speaking and listening developed by Marshall Rosenberg. It asks people to describe what happened, identify their feelings and needs, and make a specific request without treating disagreement as wrongdoing.<ref name="RosenbergIntro">Rosenberg, Marshall B. ''Nonviolent Communication: A Language of Life'', introductory chapter (2003), https://www.naturalchild.org/articles/marshall_rosenberg/giving_from_the_heart.pdf</ref>

== Four useful distinctions ==
# '''Observation:''' Say what you noticed, rather than assigning a motive. For example, "The meeting started before I arrived" instead of "You excluded me."
# '''Feeling:''' Name your own emotion, such as disappointment or anxiety, rather than guessing what another person intended.
# '''Need:''' Explain the concern beneath the feeling, such as inclusion, clarity, or safety.
# '''Request:''' Ask for a concrete, possible action while allowing the other person to decline or negotiate.

For example: "When the meeting began before I joined, I felt worried because I want to participate in decisions. Would you be willing to send me the agenda beforehand next time?" This is an '''illustration''', not a tested intervention.

== Listening matters too ==
The framework also encourages checking whether you understood another person's feelings and needs. Its purpose is not to force agreement or avoid firm boundaries. If there is coercion, violence or an urgent safety concern, communication training is not a substitute for protection, appropriate reporting or specialist assistance.<ref name="CNVCPurpose">Center for Nonviolent Communication, ''The Purpose of NVC'', https://www.cnvc.org/about/purpose-of-nvc</ref>

== Does it work? ==
Research on NVC training is mixed and methodologically uneven. A 2026 systematic review found some encouraging results for self-reported communication and empathy but weaker evidence for reducing anger and aggression. It does not prove that the method will resolve any particular conflict.<ref name="Review2026">Lazzari et al. (2026), ''Frontiers in Psychology'', doi:10.3389/fpsyg.2026.1800455.</ref>

